Announce

Payload layout and validation. For what announces are for and how they
propagate, see upstream docs/source/understanding.rst, section "The
Announce Mechanism in Detail".

Vectors: test/announce/

An announce is a packet of packet type announce. It is not encrypted.
See doc/packet for the header.


Payload

    offset  size  field
    0       64    public key
    64      10    name hash
    74      10    random hash
    84      32    ratchet, only when the context flag is set
    84/116  64    signature
    148/180 -     app data, optional

Without a ratchet the payload is at least 148 bytes, with one at least
180. Anything beyond the signature is app data.

    RNS/Destination.py:302, RNS/Identity.py:511


Random hash

Ten bytes, in two parts:

    offset  size  field
    0       5     random
    5       5     unix seconds, big endian

The timestamp is truncated to five bytes, not five bytes of a larger
field. This is why announce vectors are decode class: the same
destination announcing twice produces different bytes.

    RNS/Destination.py:283


Signed data

    signed_data = destination_hash || public_key || name_hash
                  || random_hash || ratchet || app_data

Two things differ from the wire layout, and both are easy to get
wrong.

The destination hash is signed but is not part of the payload. It is
taken from the packet header.

App data is transmitted after the signature but signed before it. On
the wire it follows the signature; in the signed data it precedes it.

Every announce vector carries the assembled signed_data field, so an
implementation that cannot verify a signature can compare its own
assembly directly.

    RNS/Destination.py:298 (sender), RNS/Identity.py:545 (receiver)


Validation

The reference implementation checks, in this order:

    1  the signature over signed_data, using the announced public key
    2  the destination hash

The second check is

    identity_hash = SHA-256(public_key)[0:16]
    expected_hash = SHA-256(name_hash || identity_hash)[0:16]
    expected_hash == destination_hash

The announce carries the name hash but not the name. A receiver
therefore learns which destination hash is claimed and can confirm it
belongs to the announced key, but cannot recover the aspect names.

    RNS/Identity.py:556, RNS/Identity.py:563


Ratchet

Thirty-two bytes, an X25519 public key, present when and only when the
context flag in the header is set. A receiver that ignores the context
flag will read the first 32 bytes of the ratchet as part of the
signature and reject every ratchet announce.

    RNS/Destination.py:287, RNS/Identity.py:525


Reproducibility of the vectors

The announces in this corpus were generated with the clock and every
source of randomness pinned, so that regenerating produces identical
bytes. That is a property of tools/gen, not of the protocol. Consumers
still cannot re-encode an announce from raw alone, because raw does
not contain the private key. The vectors are decode class.
